    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d930">

                <pb facs="0987" n="975" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg975"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.01B11 Registration—Ovakimian, Gaik
                    (Dr.)/24</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Assistant Chief of
                        the Division of European Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Henderson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-06-05">June 5, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I telephoned the Soviet Ambassador late this afternoon and informed him that the
                    appropriate officials of this Government had given careful consideration to his
                    suggestion that the State Department request that the hearings of the Ovakimian
                    case be postponed until the Soviet Embassy had completed its discussions with
                    the Department of State with regard to that case. I said that the decision had
                    been reluctantly taken that such a request on the part of the Department would
                    serve no useful purpose and that it was felt that if there was reason for the
                    postponement of the hearings of the Ovakimian case, the lawyer representing Mr.
                    Ovakimian would be the appropriate party to make a request for a
                    postponement.</p>
                <p>The Ambassador asked when he could expect a reply to the note which he had handed
                    Mr. Welles. I said that it was our hope that <pb facs="0988" n="976"
                        xml:id="pg976"/> the note of reply would reach him either this evening or in
                    the morning. Following our conversation, I have ascertained that the note had
                    already been initialed by the Secretary and is going forward to the Soviet
                    Embassy this evening.</p>
            </div>
